<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Welcome to the study of thermal properties of matter! This section will introduce you to fundamental concepts related to how substances respond to changes in temperature. Understanding these properties is crucial for comprehending various phenomena, from the workings of engines to the behavior of the atmosphere.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Definition: Heat Capacity</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Heat capacity is a fundamental property of matter that quantifies how much energy is required to raise the temperature of a substance.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Formula: Heat Capacity</div>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <div class="formula-title">Heat Capacity Formula</div>
      <div class="formula-display">
        $$
        C = \frac{Q}{\Delta T}
        $$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-explanation">
        Where:
        <ul>
          <li>\(C\) is the heat capacity (in Joules per degree Celsius, J/°C).</li>
          <li>\(Q\) is the amount of heat energy transferred (in Joules, J).</li>
          <li>\(\Delta T\) is the change in temperature (in degrees Celsius, °C).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-buttons">
        <button class="explain-button">Explain Formula</button>
        <button class="lab-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Definition: Specific Heat Capacity</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Specific heat capacity provides a standardized measure of a substance's thermal behavior by considering the mass of the substance. It represents the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of *one unit of mass* of a substance by one degree Celsius (or Kelvin). This allows for direct comparison of different materials.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Formula: Specific Heat Capacity</div>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <div class="formula-title">Specific Heat Capacity Formula</div>
      <div class="formula-display">
        $$
        c = \frac{Q}{m \Delta T}
        $$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-explanation">
        Where:
        <ul>
          <li>\(c\) is the specific heat capacity (in Joules per kilogram per degree Celsius, J/(kg·°C)).</li>
          <li>\(Q\) is the amount of heat energy transferred (in Joules, J).</li>
          <li>\(m\) is the mass of the substance (in kilograms, kg).</li>
          <li>\(\Delta T\) is the change in temperature (in degrees Celsius, °C).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-buttons">
        <button class="explain-button">Explain Formula</button>
        <button class="lab-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Heat capacity depends on the amount of substance; a larger amount requires more heat for the same temperature change.</li>
        <li>Specific heat capacity is an intrinsic property of a substance, meaning it's independent of the amount.</li>
        <li>Different substances have different specific heat capacities. For example, water has a high specific heat capacity, which is why it takes a long time to heat up or cool down.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Why is specific heat capacity a more useful concept than heat capacity for comparing different materials?</li>
        <li>How does the specific heat capacity of water influence Earth's climate?</li>
        <li>If you add the same amount of heat to two objects of equal mass, one with a high specific heat capacity and one with a low specific heat capacity, which object will experience a larger temperature change?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
```

